900	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
901	
902	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
903	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
904	
905	## Why
906	
907	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
908	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
909	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
910	
911	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
912	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
913	
914	## What this covers
915	
916	Four pieces of text, and no others:
917	
918	- the finding **title**
919	- the **Description** sentence
920	- the **Lead** description
921	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
922	
923	## What it must never touch
924	
925	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
926	
927	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
928	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
929	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
930	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
931	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
932	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
933	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
934	  never by this file.
935	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
936	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
937	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
938	
939	## The rules
940	
941	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
942	   two. Never three.
943	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
944	   sentence — cut it or split it.
945	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
946	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
947	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
948	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
949	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
950	   is unproven.
951	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
952	   second sentence.
953	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
954	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
955	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
956	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
957	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
958	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
959	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
960	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
961	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
962	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
963	   must translate before they can act.
964	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
965	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
966	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
967	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
968	    not present`.
969	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
970	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
971	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
972	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
973	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
974	
975	## Words to replace
976	
977	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
978	
979	| Do not write | Write |
980	| --- | --- |
981	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
982	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
983	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
984	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
985	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
986	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
987	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
988	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
989	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
990	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
991	| is able to | can |
992	| in order to | to |
993	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
994	| stale price | old price |
995	| sanity check | check |
996	| happy path | the normal path |
997	| atomically | in one transaction |
998	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
999	| non-zero | more than zero |
1000	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1001	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1002	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1003	
1004	## Technical names stay
1005	
1006	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1007	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1008	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1009	them.
1010	
1011	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1012	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
