894	Your `path:` and `proof:` fields are working notes, not report text. Keep them concrete;
895	concrete is already plain.
896	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
897	
898	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
899	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
900	
901	## Why
902	
903	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
904	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
905	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
906	
907	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
908	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
909	
910	## What this covers
911	
912	Four pieces of text, and no others:
913	
914	- the finding **title**
915	- the **Description** sentence
916	- the **Lead** description
917	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
918	
919	## What it must never touch
920	
921	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
922	
923	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
924	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
925	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
926	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
927	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
928	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
929	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
930	  never by this file.
931	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
932	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
933	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
934	
935	## The rules
936	
937	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
938	   two. Never three.
939	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
940	   sentence — cut it or split it.
941	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
942	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
943	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
944	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
945	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
946	   is unproven.
947	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
948	   second sentence.
949	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
950	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
951	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
952	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
953	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
954	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
955	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
956	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
957	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
958	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
959	   must translate before they can act.
960	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
961	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
962	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
963	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
964	    not present`.
965	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
966	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
967	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
968	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
969	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
970	
971	## Words to replace
972	
973	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
974	
975	| Do not write | Write |
976	| --- | --- |
977	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
978	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
979	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
980	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
981	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
982	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
983	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
984	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
985	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
986	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
987	| is able to | can |
988	| in order to | to |
989	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
990	| stale price | old price |
991	| sanity check | check |
992	| happy path | the normal path |
993	| atomically | in one transaction |
994	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
995	| non-zero | more than zero |
996	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
997	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
998	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
999	
1000	## Technical names stay
1001	
1002	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1003	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1004	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1005	them.
1006	
1007	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1008	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1009	
1010	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1011	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1012	  the same deposit twice.`
1013	
1014	## Worked examples
1015	
1016	Before, and after. The code the sentence describes did not change.
1017	
1018	**One**
1019	
1020	- Before: `Improper validation of the abort address results in a catastrophic loss of user
1021	  funds via burning to the zero address.`
1022	- After: `The function accepts a zero abort address, so a failed transfer sends the tokens to
1023	  an address that nobody controls.`
1024	
1025	**Two**
1026	
1027	- Before: `Lack of slippage protection in the swap path exposes users to sandwich attacks,
1028	  potentially leading to significant value extraction by MEV searchers.`
1029	- After: `The swap sets the minimum output to zero, so an attacker who trades before and
1030	  after the victim keeps most of the output.`
1031	
1032	**Three — a Lead**
1033	
1034	- Before: `Potential accounting inconsistency stemming from asymmetric fee handling on
1035	  deposit/withdraw flows warranting further investigation.`
1036	- After: `The deposit takes the fee from the amount, and the withdrawal takes it from the
1037	  balance. We did not find the input that makes the two disagree.`
1038	
1039	## Self-check before you write the block
1040	
1041	Four questions. Any `no` means rewrite the sentence, not the code:
1042	
1043	- Does the sentence name **who** acts and **what** they get?
1044	- Is it one sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, with no `-ing` clause?
1045	- Is every word in it a word the reader does not have to translate?
1046	- Are the identifiers, the label and the diff exactly as the source and the agent wrote them?
