910	The `group_key` enables deduplication: `ContractName | functionName | bug_class`. Agents may add custom fields.
911	
912	## Language — MANDATORY
913	
914	**Your `description:` and your `fix:` sentence are written in Simplified Technical English.** The
915	rules follow these ones in your bundle, under the heading "Report language". Read them, and
916	obey them in every finding and every lead you emit.
917	
918	Your `description:` is what the report prints. Nothing downstream rewrites it into plain
919	English for you — the orchestrator pastes it into the report and the report goes to the
920	developer who must fix the code. One sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, active voice, no
921	`-ing` clause, no metaphor. Name who acts and what they get.
922	
923	The rule reaches the wording and never the data. Your `bug_class` label, your `group_key`, the
924	contract and function names, and every line of code you quote are written exactly as the
925	source and the dedup rules require. `report-language.md` says which is which.
926	
927	Your `path:` and `proof:` fields are working notes, not report text. Keep them concrete;
928	concrete is already plain.
929	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
930	
931	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
932	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
933	
934	## Why
935	
936	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
937	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
938	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
939	
940	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
941	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
942	
943	## What this covers
944	
945	Four pieces of text, and no others:
946	
947	- the finding **title**
948	- the **Description** sentence
949	- the **Lead** description
950	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
951	
952	## What it must never touch
953	
954	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
955	
956	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
957	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
958	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
959	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
960	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
961	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
962	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
963	  never by this file.
964	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
965	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
966	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
967	
968	## The rules
969	
970	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
971	   two. Never three.
972	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
973	   sentence — cut it or split it.
974	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
975	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
976	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
977	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
978	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
979	   is unproven.
980	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
981	   second sentence.
982	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
983	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
984	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
985	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
986	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
987	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
988	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
989	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
990	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
991	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
992	   must translate before they can act.
993	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
994	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
995	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
996	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
997	    not present`.
998	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
999	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1000	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1001	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1002	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1003	
1004	## Words to replace
1005	
1006	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1007	
1008	| Do not write | Write |
1009	| --- | --- |
1010	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1011	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1012	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1013	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1014	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1015	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1016	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1017	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1018	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1019	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1020	| is able to | can |
1021	| in order to | to |
1022	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1023	| stale price | old price |
1024	| sanity check | check |
1025	| happy path | the normal path |
1026	| atomically | in one transaction |
1027	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1028	| non-zero | more than zero |
1029	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1030	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1031	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1032	
1033	## Technical names stay
1034	
1035	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1036	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1037	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1038	them.
1039	
1040	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1041	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1042	
1043	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1044	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1045	  the same deposit twice.`
1046	
1047	## Worked examples
1048	
1049	Before, and after. The code the sentence describes did not change.
1050	
1051	**One**
1052	
1053	- Before: `Improper validation of the abort address results in a catastrophic loss of user
1054	  funds via burning to the zero address.`
1055	- After: `The function accepts a zero abort address, so a failed transfer sends the tokens to
1056	  an address that nobody controls.`
1057	
1058	**Two**
1059	
1060	- Before: `Lack of slippage protection in the swap path exposes users to sandwich attacks,
1061	  potentially leading to significant value extraction by MEV searchers.`
1062	- After: `The swap sets the minimum output to zero, so an attacker who trades before and
1063	  after the victim keeps most of the output.`
1064	
1065	**Three — a Lead**
1066	
1067	- Before: `Potential accounting inconsistency stemming from asymmetric fee handling on
1068	  deposit/withdraw flows warranting further investigation.`
1069	- After: `The deposit takes the fee from the amount, and the withdrawal takes it from the
1070	  balance. We did not find the input that makes the two disagree.`
1071	
1072	## Self-check before you write the block
1073	
1074	Four questions. Any `no` means rewrite the sentence, not the code:
1075	
1076	- Does the sentence name **who** acts and **what** they get?
1077	- Is it one sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, with no `-ing` clause?
1078	- Is every word in it a word the reader does not have to translate?
1079	- Are the identifiers, the label and the diff exactly as the source and the agent wrote them?
