929	Your `path:` and `proof:` fields are working notes, not report text. Keep them concrete;
930	concrete is already plain.
931	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
932	
933	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
934	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
935	
936	## Why
937	
938	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
939	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
940	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
941	
942	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
943	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
944	
945	## What this covers
946	
947	Four pieces of text, and no others:
948	
949	- the finding **title**
950	- the **Description** sentence
951	- the **Lead** description
952	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
953	
954	## What it must never touch
955	
956	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
957	
958	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
959	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
960	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
961	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
962	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
963	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
964	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
965	  never by this file.
966	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
967	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
968	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
969	
970	## The rules
971	
972	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
973	   two. Never three.
974	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
975	   sentence — cut it or split it.
976	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
977	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
978	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
979	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
980	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
981	   is unproven.
982	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
983	   second sentence.
984	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
985	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
986	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
987	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
988	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
989	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
990	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
991	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
992	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
993	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
994	   must translate before they can act.
995	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
996	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
997	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
998	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
999	    not present`.
1000	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1001	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1002	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1003	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1004	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1005	
1006	## Words to replace
1007	
1008	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1009	
1010	| Do not write | Write |
1011	| --- | --- |
1012	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1013	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1014	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1015	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1016	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1017	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1018	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1019	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1020	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1021	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1022	| is able to | can |
1023	| in order to | to |
1024	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1025	| stale price | old price |
1026	| sanity check | check |
1027	| happy path | the normal path |
1028	| atomically | in one transaction |
1029	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1030	| non-zero | more than zero |
1031	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1032	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1033	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1034	
1035	## Technical names stay
1036	
1037	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1038	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1039	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1040	them.
1041	
1042	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1043	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1044	
1045	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1046	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1047	  the same deposit twice.`
1048	
