401	    ///    So the sender fixes the address; the owner does not enter it. One EOA calls `create` with
402	    ///    the same salt on every chain and passes the vault as `owner_`, and the clone is owned by
403	    ///    that vault from its first block at one address, with no ownership transfer ever needed.
404	    ///    Each chain is a separate contract: transferring ownership on one changes neither the salt
405	    ///    nor any other chain, so the deploying key never needs to own anything.
406	    /// 2. A second `create` with a used salt burns the whole gas limit before `DeploymentFailed`: a
407	    ///    CREATE2 collision consumes every unit forwarded to it. Read `predict(salt)` with
408	    ///    `eth_getCode` first and refuse a non-empty answer.
409	    /// 3. Same-address-everywhere is this factory's address, not a clone's claim on its own. The
410	    ///    factory is nonce-addressed where it is deployed; a byte-identical deploy through a
411	    ///    deterministic deployer is a later milestone's one-way door.
412	    function create(
413	        address owner_,
414	        uint32 delay_,
415	        string calldata description_,
416	        Distribution.Beneficiary[] calldata table_,
417	        bytes32 salt
